\pard\sb100\sa100\f2\fs24\lang16393 In any case the names of the Nakshatras used today seems to stem from the list in the Atharva Veda. Moreover, the oldest astrological texts discussing the Nakshatras preserved in China all utilise 28 nakshatra. The equal, 27 Nakshatra seems to be a later development after the infusion of hellenistic astrology and the combination of nakshatras with the rasi chakra or 12 sign zodiac. \par
