The nakshatras of traditional bhartiya astronomy are based on a list of 28 asterisms found in the Atharvaveda (AV\f3\'8c \f2 19.7) and also in the Shatapatha Brahmana.[citation needed] The first astronomical text that lists them is the Vedanga Jyotisha. In classical Hindu mythology (Mahabharata, Harivamsa), the creation of the nakshatras is attributed to Daksha. They are personified as daughters of the deity and as mythological wives of Chandra, the moon god, or alternatively the daughters of Kashyapa, the brother of Daksha. Each of the nakshatras is governed as \lquote lord\rquote  by one of the nine graha in the following sequence: Ketu (South Lunar Node), Shukra (Venus), Ravi or Surya (Sun), Chandra (Moon), Mangala (Mars), Rahu (North Lunar Node), Guru or Brihaspati (Jupiter), Shani (Saturn) and Budha (Mercury). \line In Vedic Sanskrit, the term n\'e1k\f4\u7779?\f2 atra may refer to any heavenly body, or to \ldblquote the stars\rdblquote  collectively. The classical sense of \ldblquote lunar mansion\rdblquote  is first found in the Atharvaveda, and becomes the primary meaning of the term in Classical Sanskrit.\par
\b\i Nakshatras in the Atharvavedaj\b0\i0\line In the Atharvaveda (Shaunakiya recension, hymn 19.7) a list of 28 stars or asterisms is given, many of them corresponding to the later nakshatras: (1) K\f4\u7771?\f2 ttik\f3\u257? \f2 (the Pleiads), (2) Rohin\f3\u299?\f2 , (3) Mrigash\f3\u299?\f2 rsha, (4) \f3\u256?\f2 rdr\f3\u257?\f2 , (5) Punarvasu, (6) S\f3\u363?\f2 nrit\f3\u257?\f2 , (7) Pushya, (8) Bhanu (the Sun), (9) Aslesh\f3\u257?\f2 , (10) Magh\f3\u257?\f2 , (11) Sv\f3\u257?\f2 ti (Arcturus), (12) Chitr\f3\u257? \f2 (Spica), (13), Phalgunis, (14) Hasta, (15) R\f3\u257?\f2 dhas, (16) Vish\f3\u257?\f2 kh\f3\u257?\f2 , (17) Anur\f3\u257?\f2 dh\f3\u257?\f2 , (18) Jyeshth\f3\u257?\f2 , (19) M\f3\u363?\f2 la, (20) Ash\f3\u257?\f2 dhas, (21) Abhijit, (22) Sravana, (23) Sravishth\f3\u257?\f2 s, (24) Satabhishak, (25) Proshtha-padas, (26) Revati, (27) Asvayujas, (28) Bharani. [2] Interestingly enough, the term \ldblquote nakshatra\rdblquote  has a different meaning as demonstrated in the \ldblquote Surya Siddhanta\rdblquote  which is an ancient text on astronomy.\par
Whitney on p907 implies that turmisham should be ashtavimsham ( 28 ) \line -conjectural emendation.\par
